# Task FEATURE-005-US-009-T01 — Implement: Auth security tests

> **DaemonCore coding-agent prompt.** Work type: `feature` · Size: `M` · Priority: `P2` · Status: `todo`

## Where this fits
- **Epic EPIC-005 — Identity Platform.** Introduce authentication and user ownership through a provider-agnostic abstraction, with local auth (registration, login/logout, password reset/change, profile) as the first concrete backend.
- **Feature FEATURE-005 — Identity Platform — implementation.**
- **Story FEATURE-005-US-009 — Auth security tests.** _As a maintainer, I want tests for the auth flows so security regressions are caught._

## Objective
Implement the tests for: Auth security tests.

## What to do
Write **deterministic automated tests** that cover each acceptance criterion of the parent story:
- Assert: Tests cover hashing and session lifecycle
- Assert: Reset token expiry and single-use tested
- Assert: Access scoping tested
Use fixed, hand-computed numbers (see `tests/test_fiscal_model.py`). Avoid randomness, network calls and time-dependence.

## Acceptance criteria (Definition of Done)
- [ ] Tests cover hashing and session lifecycle
- [ ] Reset token expiry and single-use tested
- [ ] Access scoping tested
- [ ] New/updated tests pass and cover the above
- [ ] No regression in the existing `pytest` suite

## Dependencies
None within this story. (Respect cross-epic dependencies of the parent epic.)

## Project conventions
- Target Python 3.13. Use British spelling in all user-facing text.
- Keep the pure model layer (`model/`) free of Streamlit; `app.py` is UI/render only.
- Pin any new runtime deps in `requirements.txt`; test-only deps in `requirements-dev.txt`.
- Write deterministic `pytest` tests; follow the style of `tests/test_fiscal_model.py`.
- Match the surrounding code style. Prefer small, coherent diffs over broad refactors.
- Update `README.md` / `docs/` whenever behaviour changes.

## Guardrails (EPIC-005)
- All auth goes through the `AuthProvider` abstraction — no coupling to one backend.
- Never store plaintext passwords; hash them. Handle sessions securely.
- Local auth is the first concrete provider; design for OIDC/social later.

## Verification
- `python3 -m py_compile <changed .py files>`
- `python3 -m pytest -q` — all tests pass (add/extend tests for this change).

